```text
CI/CD - TerTech

WHAT I BUILT:
GitHub → GitHub Actions → AWS S3 → CloudFront

STEPS:
1. Push code to main
2. GitHub Actions starts
3. Checkout repo
4. Install dependencies
5. Build Next.js app
6. Authenticate to AWS with OIDC + IAM role
7. Sync build files to S3
8. Invalidate CloudFront cache

WHY:
Automatically build and deploy every push to main without storing
long-term AWS access keys in GitHub.

KEY TERMS:
CI = Automatically build/test code when changes are pushed
CD = Automatically deploy the application after the build
OIDC = Lets GitHub securely authenticate to AWS without access keys
IAM = Controls what the GitHub workflow can access in AWS
S3 = Stores the static Next.js build
CloudFront = CDN that serves the website and caches files
CloudWatch = AWS monitoring, metrics, logs, and alarms

INTERVIEW ANSWER:

"I set up a CI/CD pipeline for my Next.js application using GitHub
Actions and AWS.

The workflow is triggered whenever I push changes to the main branch.
GitHub Actions checks out the repository, installs the dependencies,
and builds the Next.js application.

After the build succeeds, GitHub Actions uses GitHub OIDC to
authenticate with AWS and assume a specific IAM role. I chose OIDC
instead of storing AWS access keys as GitHub secrets.

The workflow then syncs the static build to an S3 bucket, which is
behind CloudFront. After deployment, it creates a CloudFront
invalidation so users receive the updated version.

I also configured CloudWatch metrics and an alarm so I can monitor
the CloudFront distribution and detect errors.

The main thing I wanted was an automated process where I could push
code to GitHub and have the application automatically build and
deploy to AWS without manually uploading files."

IF THEY ASK "WHY GITHUB ACTIONS?":

"I wanted to learn how the individual pieces of a CI/CD pipeline work
rather than relying entirely on a managed deployment platform. It
also gave me experience with AWS IAM, OIDC, S3, CloudFront, and
automated deployments."

IF THEY ASK "WHAT IS CI/CD?":

"CI is continuously integrating code changes by automatically
building and testing them. CD is continuously delivering or
deploying those changes to an environment. In my case, a push to
main triggers the build and deployment automatically."

IF THEY ASK "WHY OIDC?":

"Instead of keeping permanent AWS access keys in GitHub, OIDC allows
GitHub Actions to assume an IAM role and receive temporary
credentials. I can also restrict that role to my specific repository
and main branch."

IF THEY ASK "WHAT DOES THE IAM ROLE DO?":

"The IAM role defines what the GitHub Actions workflow is allowed to
do in AWS. I limited it to the S3 bucket and CloudFront distribution
it actually needs rather than giving it broad AWS permissions."

IF THEY ASK "WHAT HAPPENS AFTER A DEPLOYMENT?":

"The new files are uploaded to S3, then CloudFront's cache is
invalidated so the updated website can be served instead of an older
cached version."

IF THEY ASK "WHAT WOULD YOU ADD NEXT?":

"I'd add automated tests and have them run before deployment, so the
deployment only happens if the build and tests pass. For a larger
application, I could also add separate staging and production
environments."
